{
  "dom_id": "step-q327713",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Given that the expression $2(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x)$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(x+\u03d5)$ with ${0}^{\u2218}<\u03d5<{90}^{\u2218}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $\u03d5?$\n\n$\u03d5=$ \n{{field-1}}${}^{\u2218}$",
  "worked_solution": "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(x+\u03d5) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x+R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x)=R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(x+\u03d5),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n2(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5)\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x+(R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5)\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5=2 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5=2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5}{R\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5} & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}\u2061\u03d5 & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \u03d5 & =\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ \u03d5 & =60\\,{\\,}^{\u2218}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
